RDF Proxy
=========

Simple proxy application to serve collections of RDF resources from a SPARQL
endpoint as Web documents with HTTP content neogotiation.

Resource mapping approach
-------------------------

The application maps resource URIs to documents following the usual convention,
where a fragment identifier is assumed to be part of the document URI to which
the fragment is appended.

The application collects all resources that match the requested document URI,
or that are fragments on the requested document URI, and returns these.
Blank nodes are ignored for performance reasons, to make sure one request for a
document only results in one SPARQL query to the endpoint.

Proxy server support
--------------------

The following HTTP proxy headers will be taken into consideration when
identifying actual resource URIs:

* X-Forwarded-Host: Substituted for the host value when provided
* X-Forwarded-Proto: Substituted for the protocol value when provided

Dependencies
------------

* Python: https://www.python.org/
* RDFLib: https://github.com/RDFLib/rdflib
* Flask: https://github.com/pallets/flask
* mistune: https://github.com/lepture/mistune
* SPARQLx: https://github.com/lu-pl/sparqlx

Development setup
-----------------

The repository contains a Visual Studio Code Dev Containers definition,
that should contain everything necessary for developing the application.

There exists also a Podman Compose file under example/, to allow running both
Apache Jena Fuseki as a SPARQL endpoint, and the application code itself.

Configuration options
---------------------

The following environment variables are available for configuration:

* FLASK_SPARQL_ENDPOINT: The SPARQL endpoint URI to pull data from.
* FLASK_USER_AGENT: The HTTP User-Agent header value to use.
* FLASK_PREFIXES: Path to a JSON file with prefix-value mappings.

Additional Flask-specific configuration can be provided following their
documentation online.

Template mapping and context
----------------------------

The template is selected based on the types of the document URI.
For example, if the document URI is declared as having rdf:type of
schema:BlogPosting, then BlogPosting.jinja is selected as the template.

Host-specific templates are loaded from a directory under the main template
path, when such a directory exists. The directory name should be the inverse
of the host, without port number. For example, a host "127.0.0.1:8000" would
use templates from a directory named "1.0.0.127".

Errors are also converted into triples, using the schema.org vocabulary.
Errors have the type schema:Error, and use Error.jinja template.

The following variables are made available to the templates:

* graph: The output as RDFLib Graph, with identifier set to document URI
* timestamp: The current timestamp in UTC as datetime.datetime

The following custom filters are available in Jinja templates:

* sort_by_object: Sorts the input subject + object pairs by object value,
  and returns the sorted object values, with optional reverse order:

    sort_by_object(graph.subject_objects(predicate=p, distinct=True), reverse=False)

* markdown_to_html: Converts the input Markdown to HTML output using Mistune:

    graph.value(subject=s, predicate=p)|markdown_to_html|safe

